<TopicPage slug="subjects/hospitality-tourism/foundational/global-tourism">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an early lesson in Travel &amp; Tourism. It needs nothing from earlier lessons —
    only curiosity about why people move and where the money goes when they do. Everything is
    built here, step by step, from one deceptively simple question: what exactly is a tourist?
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>sort any journey by its origin — domestic, inbound, outbound — and by its purpose: leisure, business (MICE), visiting friends and relatives, or special interest;</li>
    <li>read the scale of global tourism from real numbers, and explain both the 2020 collapse and the recovery that followed;</li>
    <li>analyse demand with six families of factors, from disposable income to pandemics;</li>
    <li>explain how the package tour, the jumbo jet and the budget airline engineered mass travel;</li>
    <li>match destination features to the tourist types they appeal to, and say what seasonality and overtourism do to that appeal.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit throughout: whenever a number appears, we ask who is being counted and why they
    travelled. The counting is easy; the choosing is the subject.
  </p>

  <h2>The empty room: what tourism actually is</h2>
  <p>
    A hotel room that goes unsold tonight can never be sold for tonight. The same is true of an
    airline seat, a guide's morning, a restaurant table. Economists call tourism a
    <strong>perishable</strong> product: it cannot be stored, only sold now or lost. Keep that
    one fact in mind — it explains last-minute bargains, shoulder-season discounts, and why the
    pandemic of 2020 was not merely a bad year but a write-off of a quarter of the world's
    tourism stock.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Tourism</strong> is travel outside your usual environment for less than a
    consecutive year, for leisure, business or any other purpose. The definition is deliberately
    broad — it counts the sales representative as readily as the snorkeller — because the
    counting exists for economic reasons: who arrives, what they need, what they spend. That is
    why the first skill of this subject is not memorising categories but seeing that each
    category is a <em>point of view</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Take one journey — a Mombasa family holidaying at the Kenyan coast. To Kenya's
    statisticians this is <strong>domestic</strong> tourism: travel within your own country. Had
    the family flown to Zanzibar they would be <strong>outbound</strong> tourists leaving Kenya,
    while the Zanzibari hotel would record them as <strong>inbound</strong> tourists — arrivals
    into the country being counted — and because a border was crossed, both countries would also
    count the trip as <strong>international</strong> tourism. One holiday, three statistical
    points of view: which is why a Kenyan ministry and a German one can quote different numbers
    about the same beach, and both be right.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <em>purpose</em> of the trip changes the product being bought. A
    <strong>leisure</strong> tourist buys relaxation, sights and time. The
    <strong>business</strong> tourist of the <strong>MICE</strong> acronym — Meetings,
    Incentives, Conferences, Exhibitions/Events — buys rooms midweek, conference halls and
    reliable Wi-Fi, and typically spends more per day than a leisure guest.
    <strong>VFR</strong> travellers (visiting friends and relatives) book late, stay long, and
    spend little on hotels but much in shops. <strong>Special-interest</strong> tourists —
    birders, divers, pilgrims, festival-goers — will travel far out of season for a single
    experience no other place offers. One tourist, a different product for each.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a tourist is a foreigner on a beach holiday." The beach is
  one slice of a much larger pie. Most tourism never crosses a border — UN Tourism puts
  domestic trips at roughly three in four of all tourism trips worldwide — and business and VFR
  travel together rival leisure in value. The sales rep asleep in the airport hotel is as much
  a tourist, statistically, as the snorkeller.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> tourism is perishable travel, measured from points of view and
    sorted by origin and purpose. Next: how many such trips the world makes, and what the curve
    of that number teaches.
  </p>

  <h2>The scale: thirty years on one curve</h2>
  <p>
    In 2019 the world recorded about <strong>1.46 billion international tourist
    arrivals</strong> — arrivals meaning trips into a destination, not distinct people. Before
    the pandemic, travel and tourism supported roughly one in ten jobs on the planet and about a
    tenth of global GDP. That is why every government has a tourism policy, and why every border
    closure in 2020 made front-page economic news.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="international tourist arrivals worldwide, 1995–2023 (UNWTO) — thirty years of growth, one cliff, one rebound" widget="plotter" config={arrivalsCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> from 1995 to 2019 the blue curve more than doubles — a
    long, steady climb powered by rising disposable income and cheaper transport. Then 2020: a
    fall of roughly 72% in a single year, when the health factor overrode every other driver at
    once. The rebound to about 1.29 billion by 2023 — roughly 88% of the pre-pandemic peak —
    shows the resilience the syllabus lists among the characteristics of travel and tourism:
    the product is perishable, but the desire is durable. The same curve explains pricing: a
    seat that would otherwise expire is sold at almost any price, which is why last-minute
    bargains exist — and why a 72% collapse could not be priced away. Capacity itself had to
    shrink.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "more visitors automatically means more money." Volume and
  value are different axes. A destination can receive fewer visitors and earn more (luxury,
  longer stays, higher spend per head), or receive millions and earn comparatively little
  (cruise passengers ashore for hours, day-trippers who spend only on coffee). Destination
  managers choose which axis to grow — and the two strategies build very different resorts.</p>

  <h2>What moves demand: six families of factors</h2>
  <p>
    Why does the curve climb, dip and rebound? Syllabus 2.1(a) sorts the drivers into six
    families, and every one is visible in the chart above:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Economic</strong> — disposable income and government investment. The long climb tracks a world getting richer; the postwar decades built mass tourism's first great customer base, as steady work, weekends off and newly common paid vacations turned the annual holiday from a luxury into an expectation.</li>
    <li><strong>Social</strong> — demographics, fashion and trends; the beach holiday went from novelty to expectation within two generations.</li>
    <li><strong>Technological</strong> — developments in transport and information technology: jets, jumbos, budget fares, online booking and social media — even artificial wave pools that now put surf breaks inland.</li>
    <li><strong>Political</strong> — stability, security, terrorism and imposed limits on travel, and also agreements that remove limits: Europe's free-movement rules turned a continent into one travel market, and borders closing or reopening move the line instantly.</li>
    <li><strong>Environmental</strong> — extreme weather, natural disasters, and growing concern for the environment feeding the growth of sustainable tourism.</li>
    <li><strong>Health</strong> — pandemics above all; 2020 is the case study.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The families interact, and real demand is always several of them at once. The package
    holiday needed cheap jets (technological) <em>and</em> rising wages (economic)
    <em>and</em> paid annual leave (political — largely legislation). The Mediterranean's
    dependence on summer sun (environmental — seasonal characteristics) sets a rhythm no
    marketing can fully override. Spain's spectacular 1960s boom — a national economy partly
    fuelled by tourism — combined several at once: imported technology, government funding, and
    a rising tide of northern-European holidaymakers. Demand factors also work at every scale:
    the same six families explain why one family postpones a holiday (income) and why a nation's
    entire inbound market evaporates (borders).
  </p>
  <p>
    One driver deserves watching on its own: the growth of <strong>sustainable tourism</strong>
    (syllabus 2.1b). Changing customer attitudes, media influence, the wider availability and
    promotion of sustainable products, and government policy are shifting demand towards travel
    that protects destinations rather than consuming them — and the shift reaches deep into
    niche markets. The surfing world is a miniature of the whole industry: as warm-water breaks
    grow crowded with mass tourism, enthusiasts seek out cold-water coastlines once considered
    unappealing, wave-park technology creates inland destinations that did not exist, and
    eco-friendly board materials answer changed attitudes. Demand, not just supply, keeps
    redrawing the map of where tourism happens.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a last-minute bargain means the airline or hotel is losing
  money on me." It feels that way — surely a cheap fare cannot cover the cost of flying the
  plane? It does not have to. The aircraft costs nearly the same whether seat 24F is empty or
  occupied; the extra passenger adds only a little fuel and a cup of coffee. Any price above
  that small extra cost beats an empty seat. That is the arithmetic of a perishable product,
  not desperation.</p>

  <h2>How mass travel was engineered</h2>
  <p>
    The growth curve did not happen on its own. Each step change came from a transport or
    business innovation that cut the cost of distance — and each step moved a different
    constraint, which is why the story is worth following on a timeline.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="from a shilling rail ticket to the jumbo jet to the budget airline — how mass travel was engineered" widget="timeline" config={tourismTimeline} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four steps each remove a different barrier. Thomas
    Cook's 1841 excursion — about 500 temperance campaigners travelling from Leicester to
    Loughborough on a ticket covering train and food — proved that travel could be a
    <em>product</em>: priced, packaged and sold to people who had never left home. Vladimir
    Raitz's 1950 package (flight, transfers and a tent on Corsica for one price) proved it could
    be sold at a price the average worker could pay. The Boeing 747, entering service in 1970,
    proved distance could be industrialised: hundreds of seats per flight split the fixed cost
    of a crossing so thinly that long-haul fares fell in real terms year after year. And the
    low-cost carriers of the 2000s proved an airplane could be run like a bus route — one
    aircraft type, fast turnarounds, secondary airports, revenue from bags and seat selection.
    Cook moved <em>organisation</em>, Raitz moved <em>price</em>, the 747 moved
    <em>distance</em>, the budget airlines moved <em>frequency</em>. In sequence they invented
    mass tourism — and are the reason a 2023 flight can cost less than the taxi to the airport.
  </p>

  <h2>What makes a destination appeal</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> you can define the product and read its scale and drivers. That
    covers the demand side of the syllabus; the remaining questions are about supply — why this
    place and not that one attracts those travellers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Syllabus 2.2 turns from demand to supply: why this place and not that one? Its checklist of
    destination features is a catalogue of appeal — <strong>location; weather and seasonal
    characteristics; topographical features; accessibility; travel and transport gateways and
    hubs; infrastructure</strong> such as roads, airports and ports; <strong>built and natural
    attractions; culture</strong> (traditions, language, arts, music); expected behaviours;
    tourism organisations and providers; and sustainable provisions.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two real destinations show the checklist working in opposite directions. The Maldives sells
    weather and topography — some 1,200 coral islands and year-round warmth — but its
    accessibility is deliberately curated: international flights to one hub island, then
    seaplanes and boats. That is expensive and exclusive, and by keeping visitor numbers
    manageable per island it is also protective. Barcelona sells culture, architecture and a
    Mediterranean climate with superb accessibility — exactly the combination that produced
    <em>overtourism</em>: in some years over 30 million visitors to a city of 1.6 million,
    resident protests, and a city government capping licensed tourist beds and restricting
    cruise terminals. The features that succeeded became the management problem that followed.
  </p>
  <p>
    The analytical skill — and the exam skill — is matching features to tourist types.
    <strong>Families</strong> want safe shallow water, short transfers, family rooms and medical
    infrastructure, hence all-inclusive resorts. <strong>Business and MICE travellers</strong>
    want gateways, hubs, conference venues and schedule density, and care little for the view.
    <strong>Ecotourists</strong> want unspoiled topography, biodiversity and operators with
    credible sustainability credentials — and will pay a premium and accept discomfort for them.
    <strong>Backpackers</strong> want cheap access, hostels and social culture; cultural
    tourists follow heritage sites, festivals and food. A destination is never simply "good" —
    it is good <em>for</em> someone.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "appeal is fixed — a good beach is a good beach forever."
  Appeal is a relationship between a feature and a tourist type at a moment in time, and both
  sides move. The remote island is paradise to the ecotourist and a dealbreaker to the
  conference organiser; a culture nobody noticed becomes an attraction because a film or a feed
  made it visible (Iceland's arrivals roughly quadrupled within a decade of its viral,
  film-fuelled boom); new routes and new tastes keep re-ranking the checklist. When you analyse
  a destination, link a named feature to a named tourist type, then to how the feature is being
  managed — scale, appeal and management in one chain of reasoning.</p>

  <h2>Seasonality: the six-week year</h2>
  <p>
    One structural fact shapes every destination's business model:
    <strong>seasonality</strong>. Demand driven by weather, school calendars and festivals
    concentrates visitors into peaks, and the peak defines the economics — capacity, staffing
    and prices are set for six hot weeks while the off-season's fixed costs continue. The
    classic responses are <strong>diversification</strong> (ski resorts adding mountain biking
    and festivals; beach resorts adding conferences and wellness), <strong>pricing</strong>
    (shoulder-season discounts that fill perishable rooms) and <strong>events</strong> that
    manufacture a season where the climate did not provide one. Destination types carry their
    own rhythms too — beach and coastal, island, resort, rural and urban places peak at
    different moments — and the same journey can be a main holiday, a short break or a day trip
    depending on where the visitor starts.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A family living in Nairobi flies to Zanzibar to visit relatives. From Tanzania's statistical point of view, what kind of tourist have they become — and what purpose category is the trip?</li>
    <li>In 2020, international arrivals fell by about 72%. Why could pricing alone not rescue the year?</li>
    <li>A ski resort adds summer mountain biking and hosts conferences. Which demand problem is it treating, and what is the strategy called?</li>
    <li>Destination A receives 30 million visitors who each spend little; destination B receives 2 million who spend heavily. Which has the larger tourism economy?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) An inbound tourist — arrivals into the country being counted;
    the purpose is VFR, which is why the stay may be long and hotel spending low. (2) The
    product is perishable and the barrier was legal, not financial: with borders closed there
    was no demand at any price, so capacity itself had to shrink. (3) Seasonality, treated by
    diversification — manufacturing a demand season where the climate did not supply one.
    (4) It cannot be told from arrivals alone: value depends on spend per head and length of
    stay, which is why managers treat volume and value as separate axes. If you answered "A"
    because 30 is bigger than 2, re-read the volume–value misconception above.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Tourism is perishable: an unsold room, seat or guide-day is gone — which explains bargains, discounts and the severity of 2020.</li>
    <li>Every trip is measured from a point of view (domestic, inbound, outbound) and a purpose (leisure, business/MICE, VFR, special interest).</li>
    <li>Scale has two axes — volume (arrivals) and value (spend) — and they can move in opposite directions.</li>
    <li>Demand responds to six families of factors: economic, social, technological, political, environmental and health.</li>
    <li>Mass travel was engineered in steps: Cook (organisation), Raitz (price), the 747 (distance), budget airlines (frequency).</li>
    <li>Appeal is a relationship between destination features and tourist types — and it moves with time, access and taste.</li>
    <li>Seasonality concentrates demand into peaks; diversification, pricing and events are the standard responses.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    You can now read the map of who travels, why, and to where. The next lessons turn to the
    people who make the product: the tour operators, travel agents and tourism organisations
    that package and distribute it, and the customer service that decides whether a trip is
    remembered fondly. Later comes destination management — the craft of keeping the appeal you
    have just learned to analyse, once too many people want it.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The economic arithmetic of a destination is worth doing once, because it explains most
    policy arguments. Tourism's money <em>multiplies</em> locally: a visitor's hotel payment
    becomes wages (spent in local shops), orders to local suppliers (farmers, fishermen) and
    taxes (funding infrastructure) — the <strong>multiplier effect</strong>. But the money also
    <em>leaks</em>: if the hotel is foreign-owned, the profits, the booking commission and much
    of the food are imported, and the local gain shrinks. "How much of each tourist dollar
    stays?" is therefore the central development question, and community-based tourism exists
    as a model to keep more of it. Revenues can also pool in a coastal strip while the interior
    stays poor — leakage and concentration are separate problems.
  </p>
  <p>
    Overtourism is the management frontier: Venice charging day-trippers an access fee from
    2024 and banning large cruise ships from the lagoon's centre; Amsterdam restricting new
    hotels; Barcelona capping beds. Political integration is the quieter one: Europe's single
    market made the free movement of people — tourists included — a matter of treaty law, and
    when the United Kingdom left the European Union in 2020, Britons seeking long stays in EU
    countries needed visas again, and new paperwork appeared on goods and professional
    licences. Borders that had been invisible for a generation of travellers returned
    overnight — a live demonstration that the political factor family never stopped operating.
  </p>
  <p>
    The open question the industry is living through: is sustainable tourism a niche product or
    the next default? Aviation contributes roughly 2–3% of global energy-related carbon dioxide
    emissions, and its share grows as other sectors decarbonise; surveys consistently show a
    stated preference for sustainable travel that does not always convert into bookings at
    higher prices — the attitude–behaviour gap. Carbon pricing, sustainable aviation fuel
    mandates and rail's revival on short European routes are the live experiments. Whichever way
    they resolve, the subject's bet is clear: destinations that manage their appeal, rather than
    merely enjoying it, are the ones still appealing a generation from now.
  </p>

